WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.103
Narrator: This is a scene

00:00:01.103 --> 00:00:03.780
from the silent Western "Pinto Ben."

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:07.580
And here's what it looks like
after it's been restored.

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:11.250
All that gunk in the before
is chemical decomposition,

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:14.980
which has destroyed an estimated 75%

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:17.100
of American silent films.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.391
But here is where they get saved:

00:00:19.391 --> 00:00:21.232
the George Eastman Museum,

00:00:21.232 --> 00:00:23.480
where archivists have rescued lost works

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:27.730
of filmmakers like Stanley
Kubrick and Orson Welles.

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:30.120
One of its current projects is this:

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:32.670
a Western over a century old

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:35.150
starring and directed by William S. Hart,

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:37.670
one of the biggest
movie stars of the time.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:41.820
And it's particularly tricky
because it's made of nitrate.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:44.620
The material is notorious
for being highly flammable

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:46.350
and chemically unstable,

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:49.135
which makes it prone to decomposition.

00:00:49.135 --> 00:00:52.218
I'm sure you can start to smell this.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:58.120
It smells like nitrate
that's starting to decompose.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:00.985
My colleagues feel it
smells like a wet dog.

00:01:00.985 --> 00:01:02.450
When it gets very bad, it's more like

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:04.350
if you took a dirty gym sock

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:06.690
and put it in a glass of sour milk.

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:08.120
Narrator: While "Pinto Ben" hasn't gotten

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:09.640
to the point of sour milk,

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:12.650
the clear film base has
started to discolor.

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:14.130
That's stage one.

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:16.410
Anthony: As you wind
through it, you could see

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:20.060
how it's becoming more
and more brownish-yellow.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:23.180
This was stage two, where the
image has started to melt.

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:24.330
Narrator: This is where the emulsion

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:26.030
in the film has broken down.

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:28.660
Anthony: The third stage,
the film starts to ooze

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:31.420
and bubble like a honey-like substance.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:33.920
Up to that point, parts of
it can still be salvaged.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:37.100
And then stage four, it
crystallizes into a hockey puck.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:40.660
After that, it becomes a
very fine brown powder.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:42.690
Narrator: That process is irreversible,

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:45.010
and it can happen surprisingly fast,

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:46.702
so archivists like Anthony

00:01:46.702 --> 00:01:48.870
have to identify decomposing films

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:52.500
and restore them to new film
stock before it's too late.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:53.880
In silent films like this,

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.430
they may start looking
around the title cards.

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.780
Titles usually were photographed

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:01.810
and processed more poorly than the images.

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:04.830
A lot of the decomp can
start from the titles

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:07.130
and then work its way
through the rest of the film.

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:09.760
Narrator: Then they'll
start on physical repairs.

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:11.870
Each of these splices
marks a tear in the film

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:15.170
that an archivist fused back
together with liquid cement

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:18.470
or damaged frames that had to
be replaced with blank film.

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:19.700
Some of these repairs were done

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:22.430
before the reel came into
Eastman's possession.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:25.990
Another area that needs
mending: these perforated edges.

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:27.910
If those perforations get ripped off,

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:31.690
the tear can spread inwards
and begin to affect the image.

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:34.011
Also, those perforations, when intact,

00:02:34.011 --> 00:02:36.390
allow the film to move
safely through the cleaner

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:38.270
and scanner in the next steps.

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:40.500
So in areas with extensive damage ...

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:42.920
Anthony: The edge and the
perfs are missing completely.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:44.750
Narrator: Anthony will
use this special tape

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:47.270
to replace the missing perforations.

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:48.520
By the end of this process,

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:50.560
the film will look something like this,

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:54.260
with all those white stripes
marking perforation repairs.

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:57.010
Once all those weakened
areas have been reinforced,

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:59.310
the film goes through
this ultrasonic cleaner

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:02.020
to remove any dust or dirt on the surface.

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:04.620
Then the film is finally digitized.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:05.900
It goes through the scanner,

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:09.600
which captures each frame and
turns it into a digital file.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.400
This process can take up to
48 hours for a two-hour film,

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:14.690
and the team has to do this

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:16.206
for every version of the film

00:03:16.206 --> 00:03:18.714
they're going to use
for the reconstruction.

00:03:18.714 --> 00:03:22.380
Eastman is reconstructing
"Pinto Ben" from six sources,

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:25.860
with each providing different
puzzle pieces of the film.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:27.162
Two of the sources come from

00:03:27.162 --> 00:03:29.370
the 1917 reissue of the film

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:31.653
under the title "Horns and Hoofs."

00:03:31.653 --> 00:03:34.070
The Library of Congress
preserved the nitrate

00:03:34.070 --> 00:03:36.330
for reel one, the first half of the film,

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:37.963
and the negative for reel two,

00:03:37.963 --> 00:03:39.738
the second half of the film.

00:03:39.738 --> 00:03:42.240
These sources have the
best image quality --

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:45.360
the shots look sharp and
have good contrast --

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.210
but the Library of Congress nitrate

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:49.580
is the least complete element.

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:52.100
These frames have some
yellow ooze on the side,

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:54.950
which marks the beginning
of the decomposition,

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:57.890
while these frames show
it in its later stages.

00:03:57.890 --> 00:03:59.990
Graham: We cannot digitally restore this

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:02.730
because there is very
little image information

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:04.500
for our software to look at,

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:05.700
so we're going to want to use that

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:07.270
for as many scenes as we can,

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:09.870
but if frames are missing or if the decomp

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:14.320
and the damage is so
distracting and irreconcilable,

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:17.500
we'll switch to the other
nitrate print that we have.

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:19.730
Narrator: This nitrate
was sent over from France,

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:22.980
where "Pinto Ben" was released
as "Le Poney de Rio Jim."

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:25.007
Bryan: Some pieces were actually excised

00:04:25.007 --> 00:04:26.265
throughout the years,

00:04:26.265 --> 00:04:28.690
maybe because of decomp
or damage, we don't know.

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:30.540
Narrator: To fill in chunks
that are still missing,

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:32.358
the team will defer to one of three

00:04:32.358 --> 00:04:36.340
lower-res prints of the
film from a 1924 re-release.

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:39.540
Altogether, these prints
contain the most information,

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:42.290
since they have the highest
number of frames intact.

00:04:42.290 --> 00:04:45.670
But the image quality here
is probably the roughest.

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:48.760
Graham: You can see the focus
is a little bit fuzzier,

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:51.450
it's a little bit flatter,
the contrast isn't as good.

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:53.460
Narrator: They can play with
the contrast to help correct

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:56.100
for any obvious quality
jumps between frames,

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:59.013
and color grading will help
tie everything together.

00:04:59.013 --> 00:05:00.560
Graham: And then from all
those different elements

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:01.720
and all those different frames,

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:04.960
we assemble as complete
a version as possible.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:06.870
Narrator: Once a timeline
of all the best shots

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:10.220
has been created, digital
restoration begins.

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:12.940
The first thing to correct
is this shaky movement

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:14.360
you see between frames.

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:17.400
Often it's the result of
film shrinking over time.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:19.740
Shrunken film doesn't go
through the scanner as well,

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:22.030
so you can end up with an unstable image.

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:24.480
Chris: We know in analog
film, there's a little bit

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:26.920
of a slight vibration in the film image,

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:30.280
but if it were to regularly
distractingly move up and down,

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:32.360
we would try and eliminate
that to a degree.

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:34.260
The thing that this element suffers from

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:35.605
is mostly scratching.

00:05:35.605 --> 00:05:37.190
Narrator: Software helps detect these,

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:39.050
but it's not a perfect solution.

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:40.600
Chris: In this film,
there's a lot of wallpaper

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:41.810
and a lot of vertical lines

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:43.500
that resemble these types of scratches,

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:46.690
so I put in a scratch that's very thick,

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:47.573
it's going to say,

00:05:47.573 --> 00:05:49.514
hey, I'm this line on
the wallpaper, right?

00:05:49.514 --> 00:05:50.840
I should be removing that.

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:53.215
Narrator: He can use the same
kind of programmed filters

00:05:53.215 --> 00:05:55.740
to help detect dark dirt, light dust,

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:58.780
or other errant artifacts,
like the stray eyelash

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:01.400
or hair that may have become
embedded into the print.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:03.390
Chris: So that's just
a piece of errant dirt

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:05.660
that fell onto the film at some point.

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:07.170
Narrator: Other artifacts
might be captured

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:08.430
in the image itself,

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:09.890
for example, if there was dirt

00:06:09.890 --> 00:06:11.500
on the printer or a camera negative

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:13.880
when the film was printed
however many years ago.

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:15.350
You tend to find the most dirt

00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:17.140
and dust at the ends of the film.

00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:19.220
Bryan: Those would be
exposed to the elements more.

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:20.253
Just sitting in a can,

00:06:20.253 --> 00:06:21.520
it would be either heads out or tails out,

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:22.630
so that would accumulate dust

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:24.850
when you're putting it in
the can, taking it out.

00:06:24.850 --> 00:06:27.140
Narrator: In general, the
more popular a film was

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:29.070
and the more times it
was shown in theaters,

00:06:29.070 --> 00:06:30.780
the more likely it is to have suffered

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:34.430
extensive wear and tear, from
scratches and thumbprints

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:37.440
to spots like this, where
the emulsion's rubbed off,

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:40.130
a common effect of repeated projection.

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:43.170
The last step in the
process: color grading.

00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:44.970
Here, the team has the scanned frames

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:46.800
of "Pinto Ben" up on the screen

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:49.590
and the nitrate film out
on the inspection table.

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:52.033
The goal is to make the
colors on that screen

00:06:52.033 --> 00:06:53.783
match those in the physical reference

00:06:53.783 --> 00:06:55.841
as closely as possible.

00:06:55.841 --> 00:06:59.220
This takes a lot of
precision and teamwork.

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:00.110
Bryan: Warmer, colder?

00:07:00.110 --> 00:07:01.380
Graham: I would say warmer,

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:03.570
but I feel like we've lost
some of the saturation.

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:07.020
It's a very deep, vivid,
almost radioactive yellow.

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:08.650
Narrator: They have to be very gentle

00:07:08.650 --> 00:07:09.610
with a print like this

00:07:09.610 --> 00:07:12.350
as they go through and inspect
the color in each scene.

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:14.208
Graham: As you can see,
the film is bending

00:07:14.208 --> 00:07:16.687
in one direction, as
well as these edges here

00:07:16.687 --> 00:07:18.200
are buckling out a little bit.

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:20.850
Those are all signs of warpage

00:07:20.850 --> 00:07:23.590
and physical deterioration
of the film stock.

00:07:23.590 --> 00:07:24.880
If that gets any worse,

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:27.150
this element might not be able
to be scanned in the future.

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:30.520
And so when we inspect it, we
move through it very slowly,

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:31.818
because the last thing we want to do

00:07:31.818 --> 00:07:34.960
is to introduce more
damage to the film print.

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:36.210
Narrator: At the end of the process,

00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:38.247
the film looks like this --

00:07:38.247 --> 00:07:40.113
pretty close to how it was originally seen

00:07:40.113 --> 00:07:42.980
by audiences back in 1915.

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:44.350
And that's the goal.

00:07:44.350 --> 00:07:47.880
We're trying to present
the film as an artifact

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:51.490
and an art form the way it
was originally presented.

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:54.670
Narrator: This whole process
can take up to two years,

00:07:54.670 --> 00:07:57.750
which means many movies
are deteriorating faster

00:07:57.750 --> 00:07:59.860
than archives can preserve them.

00:07:59.860 --> 00:08:02.530
If we have a unique print where we know

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:05.200
that this is the last
surviving element of a film

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:08.140
and we notice that it's
starting to decompose,

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:11.317
that will go to the top of our
preservation priority list.

00:08:11.317 --> 00:08:13.958
Narrator: "Pinto Ben" is one
of nine William S. Hart films

00:08:13.958 --> 00:08:17.405
Eastman is restoring, and
one of thousands of movies

00:08:17.405 --> 00:08:20.790
the museum's helped rescue
from being lost forever.

